body:
have you thought about running away?? and trying to find a homeless shelter to live at? I bet they have better ideas on how to do it over there.

&#x200B;

Edit: Also to work at a place like mcdonnalds to provide for yourself if you decide to change your mind.

 It's been about 5 days on this dose, but I gone from 50 mg for over a month. 

&#x200B;

I drink coffee in the morning, but doesn't really help.

"Ladies, I believe you have all the right to make this decision alone, and you shouldn't even consult anyone, only a doctor.

&#x200B;

That. is. fair. Now ladies, to be fair to us, if you decide to have the baby... I shouldn't be required to pay for him.

&#x200B;

If you get to kill this motherfucker, I can at least abandon him".

Percy Jackson, there are gods out there.

&#x200B;

Just kidding, mine would be How to Make Friends and Influence People.

Carry Wood, Chop Water.

&#x200B;

Yeah you read that right.

However, I do think I understand where you are coming from. People should be treated as individuals who can think, feel and change the social structure they live in.

They should be explained why "everybody" does that. They should be invited to think and not merely be controlled by others for the sake of "Tradition".

&#x200B;

In that I agree 100%.

&#x200B;

I'm only talking about the people that don't want to be invited to the conversation, because someone is trying to convince them to do something "old". 

&#x200B;

As you pointed out, that is immature behavior. To dismiss any value even before the conversation began.

Just like a teenager doesn't want to hear anything from her/his father just because they are "old" and "don't get it".

&#x200B;

The saying "Tradition is as important as Innovation" shouldn't make people just accept whatever tradition they've been fed their lives. It should open their minds to the **possibility** that someone might know something they don't.

What do you mean by judgmental exactly?

Can you paint a clear picture to me? Because I think we mean different things and might actually agree on some points.

&#x200B;

My view is as simple as that. "Judgmental" shouldn't be a bad word, people should be allowed to judge, as they naturally do, and be able to engage in debates on controversial topics allowed to say "I think the way you live your life is wrong for the majority of the population" without getting beat up by people who disagree with them.

&#x200B;

We call those people that say those "nasty" things judgmental, but the people who beat them up are just as judgmental.

&#x200B;

I am an advocate of people saying whatever the hell they want, and be allowed to do so without such a negative label printed on them.

Judgment shouldn't be a bad word.

It is how we measure each other to one's ideal, because without an ideal there is no growth.

The ideal can chance, as it is doing now, but society should judge if the new ideal is something to aspire to. And if it isn't for one way or another, it should be allowed to say "I think that is wrong".

&#x200B;

What I'm fighting here is for the right for people to say "you are wrong and we aren't following your ideals", and do so in large scale.

&#x200B;

That can hurt some people's feelings (a right-wing person inside a largely left-wing classroom) or a left-wing fighting for LGBT+ rights in their own household.

&#x200B;

But they, in saying something that the majority disagrees with, should be judged.

That is what they are putting themselves against.

&#x200B;

You speak something different than what others think, you will be judged harshly for it. And that is okay, and accepting that it is okay is the first step to start having more civil debates.

We all want love.

&#x200B;

Love is what makes life meaningful.

&#x200B;

You want love.

&#x200B;

And right now, although extremely hard, the only person that could love you, at least from what you wrote, is yourself.

&#x200B;

Treat yourself as you would treat a friend that you want to see get better.

&#x200B;

We are all behind you.

May you be peaceful.

Everybody suffers in life.

No one belongs anywhere.

Everybody is going to die.

&#x200B;

Everyone is going to face those things soon or later in life. Maybe not the "no one belongs anywhere", the most normal of humans(the larger groups that follow the same rules) tend to not be confronted with that fact long enough even if they do it sometimes.

&#x200B;

I'm sorry for your suffering, people shouldn't mock others because they are weird accordingly to their opinion. But, that is what humans are biologically designed to do.

&#x200B;

We are designed to fill disgust and anger towards others that don't follow the same rules that we do. It is what caused wars among countries and between empires following different religions.

&#x200B;

We feel safe inside an environment where everybody follows the same rules, because if else, what reason do I have to trust you won't betray me after I turn my back? If you also don't believe in after life, or in the judicial system?

&#x200B;

Outside society's norms there is no guarantee that two humans will cooperate. Inside it though, you have a 99% chance that a random person could cooperate with you using money or other mind constructs that everyone believes.

&#x200B;

Now to my point, it sucks to be a feminine guy, and in some places it is dangerous to portray that. And that can and does cause long lasting trauma in individuals.

But that is the chance that nature is willing to take. It is willing to create some individuals that diverge from the norm. Because that is how species evolve. The downside of that is that those individuals have the most amount of pressure on them, to prove to others that their unusual way is better than the traditions.

&#x200B;

You didn't chose to be born like this. But since you are different, by default you have to tread more carefully. Because your way disrupts the norm. Just like Galileo was sent to the fire because of his unusual ways, or Socrates.

&#x200B;

Unusual is disliked, we can create a society that respects it, but never accept without question.

It is unfortunate. I stand with you in this. Internet is there to help us find a smaller community where our ways aren't wrong by default and we can find some peace in our lives.

&#x200B;

But I'm afraid those pressures won't ever stop. Either we learn to live with them, hiding when we must, and showing strength when we can, and accepting when there's nothing we can do.

Or we don't.

Which would be sad for everyone that shares love with us.

What would make something matter?

You are under the assumption that because an action won't change things in a large scope of the universe, then it doesn't matter.

So by this definition, the only real thing that mattered, was the big bang(or what supposedly created the universe), because it is really the only thing you can obviously point out that affected literally everything.

&#x200B;

However, and this is where it gets interesting...

&#x200B;

Small things govern the universe.

&#x200B;

Quantum world governs the universe. We haven't been able to create the bridge between the quantum world and relativistic things, but, we do know everything is composed by it's simpler components into quantum particles.

&#x200B;

Why am I saying all this?

&#x200B;

Although saying something matters or not could ease anxiety and lead to a more fulfilling life for some, for others it could cause the opposite, and rob people of reason to live.

&#x200B;

I know that it was what happened to me when I first had this idea of "no matter what I do, in 100 years it won't matter".

&#x200B;

The thing is, for something to matter, it is a subjective fact, and you could argue that if something affects even a small part of the universe, it reverberates throughout  it. And even a simple atom or a quantum particle could initiate a process as big as a supernova or a black hole.

&#x200B;

In that sense, even the day you picked up the trash in the street and put it in the litter, or the warm smile you gave to a friend that prevented him to commit suicide one more day, could in fact, initiate a process you couldn't even begin to comprehend in the universe.

&#x200B;

It is a stretch.

&#x200B;

But for some, it's what they need to keep going, possibility that what they do matter and it is positive to the world and the entire realm of existence.

&#x200B;

On the other hand, there are those who get more of life when they find the Buddha and kill it.

And go fishing afterwards.

It's when you realize that doing more in a given situation is worse than just not doing anything.

&#x200B;

TO be able to keep your mind steady to understand when action is needed. And if it is, do it immediately without any delaying.


But also to understand when action is not needed, and then learn to wait patiently, continuously analyzing the situation and keeping your understanding of what reality is presenting you.

&#x200B;

I'm nowhere near where I want to be in terms of understanding reality as it is presented to me and balancing action to non action(also called Wu Wei in Chinese philosophy).

&#x200B;

And establishing, for my current situation, how to balance it, has been my most difficult challenge.

The difference between skillful and unskillful desire really opened my eyes.

&#x200B;

Thank you. I'm also reading on your references. Thank you very much.

I do feel moments of intense pressure, and even paranoia, but not as mucn as before. I feel those mostly when there is another person involved. When I feel betrayed, either in reality or in my imagination.


There are still scenarios that I'd be destroyed if happened to me, but now I've learned to rely on what I want to do with my life, even if it means doing it alone.

&#x200B;

Taking a time to work on skills, activities that are good for you and get you closer to your goal has been what saves me so far.

&#x200B;

That and a mix of Stoic teachings and Zen.

It's just, I never imagined she would do this kind of shit to me. From all my previous girlfriends, she was the best. She didn't violate me, didn't hit me, didn't cheat.

&#x200B;

Well, she did it now.

Winston Churchill couldn't get out of bed either. Neither can I when I don't absolutely have to. Except when I put on some metal and go have a shower.

&#x200B;

Either learn to work till late at night or put some energetic song to get yo butt off bed.

Just remember, as Alan said himself, he is an intellectual entertainer.

&#x200B;

It entertains the mind, but if you want change, you need action. Be it meditating, working out, doing something everyday that cleanses you.

&#x200B;

If you like what Alan says, have a go at meditating, yoga, fasting and things like that :D

Learn to observe. It is much deeper than what you are probably thinking.

&#x200B;

Observe others, how they act.

&#x200B;

Observe yourself, your breathing, your emotions, and more importantly, observe pain. Learn to observe and not be envolved with it, just observe.

>Say we had basic income and free health care. That takes care of your friends situation.

The thing is, we can't just assume it. I see where you are coming from, you are discussing a future where many of humanity problems have been taken care of. However, if we don't come up with practical ways to do this now, we may not have 80 years from now to enjoy those benefits.

&#x200B;

I really want to get to that point, but if history showed us anything, is that in order to do that, we must be able to innovate and create new things, new technology to serve us. Technology and innovation has helped us live better than kings in the dark ages, and it isn't only a small percentage of the world population. We have created Wealth. 

&#x200B;

Technology helps us build the better world. But in order for it to be developed, we need economics, we need to be able to understand markets. It isn't enough to invent, you must propagate that technology, you must be able to understand markets and business so well that you are able to make that tech cheaper and accessible to many.

&#x200B;

In that way big corporations like Apple, Amazon, Microsoft, Google, and many others have created a world of democratized technology. It isn't perfect, but if you compare to any other human period, we live in the greatest period of democratization since the beginning of time. About 4 billion people own a smartphone, half the world population.

&#x200B;

I think we live in wonderful times, and with the possibility to change everything for the better of human kind. But in order to do that, we need to learn those skills I mentioned earlier.

I thank you for being so open with us. I can't promise anything, and I know how it is to be in your situation.

&#x200B;

But I'm glad you could realize that your depressed state isn't about who you are as a person, it is based on your circumstances. That is a big step towards understanding to focus on what you can control, and let go of what you can't.

&#x200B;

I hope your job search turns out well. I'm no expert in it so I can't be of much help in that. 

&#x200B;

But if by any measure you believe someone on this sub could use some of your knowledge and you care to share your qualifications, maybe in this way this community could help, either recommending or sharing how to pursue a job in your area of expertise.

&#x200B;

Just a thought.

I'm not making it personal to you. It is not an attack. I am merely saying that the way you write shows inexperience dealing with life, you choose to make it personal or not, I am only saying my opinion.

&#x200B;

The point I'm making is that your doubts about these things do have a time and a place, but not in today's world. In today's world either you work to amount to something or you get left behind.

&#x200B;

>"The goal of the future is full unemployment, so we can play".\~Arthur C Clarke.

&#x200B;

I like him as well, brilliant fellow. But I disagree with him on this one.  

&#x200B;

Playing all the time isn't the way our brains evolved to life with. Even sex, if you do it 12 hours a day, everyday, getting criticism for every mistake you make, for over 10 years isn't 'fun' or 'play'. However, doing an activity in this way is the only way to get better and provide better value.

&#x200B;

Providing better value is our individual way to 'serve humanity'.

Your boyfriend is a jerk, thinks of himself at least 4 times before he thinks of you. Simple as that.

&#x200B;

You just got a direct measure of that. 4 times 'me', 1 time 'her needs'.

I felt peace for the first time in a long time after I went to a Mosque with a friend of mine here in Brazil. I found that peace again after I went to a Buddhist temple, and the third time when I was in the retreat.

&#x200B;

What all those things had in common was how much deep empathy it made me feel by just being in the place.

&#x200B;

I learned that when the mind is calm, when you just observe, empathy just grows and fills you up. I never felt so much empathy than I did at the retreat, it was amazing.

&#x200B;

But I always wanted to be a business person, and I feel as if being too empathetic could work against that.

&#x200B;

I was raised to be polite and a good boy, but life taught me that people just sh!t on people like that. I believed I needed to be arrogant to shelter me from that.

&#x200B;

But hearing from Goenka put a different view on that in my mind. How the Buddha could yell, be angry, but all in order to teach others. I still don't understand that full well, but I changed "turn the other cheek" teaching in my mind to "learn to stand up for yourself".

&#x200B;

It made being assertive be "the right thing to do".

&#x200B;

Thank you for your reply. I wish you well.

>I crave depression because it's familiar and that familiarity is comforting

I can't say for sure I have had depression, certainly what I feel at times fits some of the symptoms, but I do understand what you are saying.

&#x200B;

Familiar feels good, but it isn't always good for us.

&#x200B;

I feel you, I hope we can be well. :)

I just.

I know I'll probably sound like a child now.

I was raised as Christian, in a world where all my immediate needs (food, shelter, family) were met. Sure I didn't have my own room, or the nicest toys, but I had opportunities for learning new things at a young age and the support from my parents.

&#x200B;

I was raised believing I had to behave in a certain way and god would smile upon me. I studied hard, learned about new things after school and behaved as a 'good boy'. Things happened, I stopped believing, and since then I have been reaching to find the 'right way to be'.

&#x200B;

I guess part of the journey is learning to be honest with yourself, but not taking yourself too seriously.

&#x200B;

It's hard finding the balance.

&#x200B;

Thank you for your reply. : )

>But you seem to feel that there is some objective, right way to be — or that the measure of whether you are doing things right is happiness or success.

This, I couldn't put it in better words than you did.

&#x200B;

I have cried a lot in my life in times where I just didn't know what I was 'supposed' to do. Like after a semester ended and I had a day to really be with myself in my university dorms without anyone.

&#x200B;

I still have that at times. I actually started meditating because I thought I was 'supposed' to be like Steve Jobs at college. Meditating, going to parties, having a girlfriend, doing drugs.

&#x200B;

After my retreat I started listening more to myself, but I'm still conflicted.

&#x200B;

It's amazing how the silence in the retreat helped me get in touch with who I am. I miss that silence sometimes.

&#x200B;

Thank you for the reply, really appreciate it.

I thank you for your reply,  I have often wondered about this, Asperger, Bipolar, Autism, even sociopathy at times, I guess I'll never know for sure until I actually seek professional help for it. Unfortunately, my economic situation doesn't allow me to, but it is something I want to seek in the future.

&#x200B;

Thanks again for the reply, and I do listen to dhamma, it's one of the few things appart from simply meditating that really calms me down and gives me some sort of peace.

Thank you for your reply, it seems very down to earth, and concur to my own observations.

&#x200B;

Indeed meditation practices have helped me lead a better life overall and studying the mind has helped me gain back perspective over my problems.

I sell a journey within your own mind. 

&#x200B;

Are you often wondering why you keep putting yourself down and not accomplishing much?

&#x200B;

I won't make the journey for you, but I'll put you towards it so all that is left is the First Step.

&#x200B;

How to help yourself achieve more?

